"Less Diverse, Less Safe:The Indirect But Pervasive Risk of Test-Time Scaling in Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.08592v1)\nciteturn2816view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.08592v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 345\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Works L18:   4. cite8†3 Problem Statement and Proposed Stress Test L19:     1. cite9†3.1 Preliminaries L20:       1. cite10†Attack Success Rate (ASR). L21:     2. cite11†3.2 RefDiv: The Proposed Reference-Guided Diversity Stress Test Protocol L22:   5. cite12†4 Experiments and Results L23:     1. cite13†4.1 Experimental Setup L24:     2. cite14†4.2 Main Results L25:     3. cite15†4.3 Why Does RefDiv Work? L26:     4. cite16†4.4 Transferability Across TTS Strategies L27:     5. cite17†4.5 Transferability To Closed-Source LLMs L28:     6. cite18†4.6 Transferability To Guardrails/Safety Classifiers L29:   6. cite19†5 Conclusion L30:   7. cite20†References L31:   8. cite21†A Experiments with Best-of-$N$ for Different Values of n L32:   9. cite22†B Shannon Entropy trends for MCTS L33:   10. cite23†C Additional Experiments with Reward Models L34:   11. cite24†D Implementation Details L35: cite25†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L36: \nL37: arXiv:2510.08592v1 [cs.CL] 04 Oct 2025\nL38: # Less Diverse, Less Safe:\nL39: The Indirect But Pervasive Risk of Test-Time Scaling in Large Language Models Thanks: Email: {shahriarkabir, anshumanc}@usf.edu Thanks: Email: {haskari, muhchen}@ucdavis.edu\nL40: Shahriar Kabir Nahin Affiliation: Bellini College of AI, Cybersecurity, and Computing, University of South Florida    Hadi Askari Affiliation: Department of Computer Science, University of California, Davis    Muhao Chen Affiliation: Department of Computer Science, University of California, Davis    Anshuman Chhabra Affiliation: Bellini College of AI, Cybersecurity, and Computing, University of South Florida\nL41: ###### Abstract\nL42: Test-Time Scaling (TTS) improves LLM reasoning by exploring multiple candidate responses and then operating over this set to find the best output. A tacit premise behind TTS is that sufficiently diverse candidate pools enhance reliability. In this work, we show that this assumption in TTS introduces a previously unrecognized failure mode. When candidate diversity is curtailed, even by a modest amount, TTS becomes much more likely to produce unsafe outputs.\nL43: We present a reference-guided diversity reduction protocol (RefDiv) that serves as a diagnostic attack to stress test TTS pipelines. Through extensive experiments across four open-source models (Qwen3, Mistral, Llama3.1, Gemma3) and two widely used TTS strategies (Monte Carlo Tree Search and Best-of-$N$), constraining diversity consistently signifies the rate at which TTS produces unsafe results. The effect is often stronger than that produced by prompts directly with high adversarial intent scores.\nL44: This observed phenomenon also transfers across TTS strategies and to closed-source models (e.g. OpenAI o3 and Gemini-2.5-Pro), thus indicating that this is a general and extant property of TTS rather than a model-specific artifact. Additionally, we find that numerous widely used safety guardrail classifiers (e.g.\nL45: Llama-Guard and OpenAI Moderation API), are unable to flag the adversarial input prompts generated by RefDiv, demonstrating that existing defenses offer limited protection against this diversity-driven failure mode. Through this work, we hope to motivate future research on designing robust TTS strategies that are both effective and secure against diversity-targeted stress tests as illustrated by RefDiv.\nL46: ## 1 Introduction\nL47: Large Language Models (LLMs) have become central to a wide range of applications, from content generation to complex problem-solving (cite26†Naveed et al., 2025 ).\nL48: LLMs are now used in most tasks in Natural Language Processing (NLP), such as Conversational Agents (cite27†Ouyang et al., 2022 ; cite28†Wang et al., 2023 ; cite29†Zhang et al., 2020 ), Content Generation (cite30†Madotto et al., 2020 ), Code Generation (cite31†Islam et al., 2024 ), Content Analysis (cite32†Kocmi and Federmann, 2023 ), Fact Checking (cite33†Lewis et al., 2021 ), etc. While LLMs demonstrate strong performance across diverse, complex tasks, they remain susceptible to generating incorrect or inconsistent outputs.\nL49: Recent work on Test-Time Scaling (TTS) methods has shown that allowing models to generate and evaluate multiple candidate responses at inference time can improve output quality and reliability significantly (cite34†Yao et al., 2023 ; cite35†Wei et al., 2022 ). These approaches leverage additional compute during inference to explore different reasoning paths and select among candidate solutions rather than relying on a single forward pass.\nL50: TTS methods range from efficient sampling-based methods such as Best-of-$N$ selection (cite36†Cobbe et al., 2021 ), where multiple independent responses are generated and filtered according to consistency or scoring criteria, to structured prompting methods that guide the model to decompose problems systematically (cite35†Wei et al., 2022 ; cite34†Yao et al., 2023 ) and explore multiple reasoning paths in a tree structure. More sophisticated approaches frame inference as search over a solution space of candidates.\nL51: For instance, recent work has adapted Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) (cite37†Coulom, 2006 ; cite38†Gao et al., 2024 ; cite39†Inoue et al., 2025 ) to guide LLM reasoning by treating generation as sequential decision-making, enabling systematic exploration and backtracking through potential solution paths.\nL52: Despite all the developments aimed at increasing the robustness of LLMs, they remain vulnerable to adversarial inputs that can induce unintended behaviors. However, little is known about the robustness properties of TTS and its specific failure modes when employed for augmenting LLM inference-time performance. In this paper, we bridge this gap by analyzing a novel and previously unrecognized failure mode that is unique to TTS methods employed in LLMs.\nL53: More specifically, the effectiveness of TTS depends critically on the diversity of the candidate response distribution, where diverse samples enable better exploration of the solution space and more robust selection mechanisms. We thus stress test TTS robustness by exploring this reliance on diversity in our work: by simply constraining the candidate pool to be homogenous (i.e. containing low diversity), TTS outcomes can be easily steered to generate harmful responses.\nL54: That is, we hypothesize that constraining response diversity represents a key indirect but pervasive vulnerability in TTS systems. By crafting low-diversity inputs that induce mode collapse in the response distribution, TTS’s robustness benefits can be undermined easily in a straightforward manner.\nL55: To this end, we propose RefDiv, or the Reference-Guided Diversity Stress Test Protocol, which specifically targets the diversity of intermediate responses in TTS pipelines, and leads to significantly higher robustness lapses across various LLMs and TTS strategies, compared to state-of-the-art jailbreak attacks. Moreover, the adversarial strings generated by RefDiv transfer successfully across TTS strategies, closed-source models, as well as guardrail classifiers (e.g.\nL56: Llama-Guard and OpenAI Moderation API) further underscoring the need for improving the robustness of TTS-based LLM frameworks.\nL57: Contributions. In sum, we make the following key contributions in this work:\nL58: \nL59:   * •\nL60: \nL61: We demonstrate a novel failure mode in TTS-based LLMs that leverages diversity of the candidate solutions, through our proposed RefDiv stress test protocol. RefDiv seeks to reduce the diversity of the candidates generated during test-time while steering them towards harmful generations, ultimately resulting in TTS producing unsafe results (at higher rates compared to state-of-the-art attack baselines).\nL62: \nL63:   * •\nL64: We extensively validate RefDiv across different TTS strategies (MCTS and Best-of-$N$), and several LLMs of different types (Qwen3, Mistral, Llama3.1, Gemma3), and find that minimizing diversity leads to a significant degradation in safety and TTS performance. Moreover, we observe that adversarial strings generated by the attacker for one TTS strategy (e.g. MCTS) can be used to attack another (e.g.\nL65: Best-of-$N$) indicating that this phenomenon is a byproduct of general TTS frameworks and not specific to the models.\nL66:   * •\nL67: \nL68: Furthermore, we find that the diagnostic prompts RefDiv generates easily transfer to closed-source LLMs (such as GPT-4.1, o3-mini, Gemini-2.5-Flash, and Gemini-2.5-Pro), leading to unsafe/harmful generations even when the target model is unknown. This demonstrates the potential of RefDiv as a stress test tool even when models are only available via black-box access.\nL69: \nL70:   * •\nL71: Finally, to analyze whether current state-of-the-art guardrail/safety classifiers can flag RefDiv’s stress-test inputs, we employ Llama-Guard-3, Llama-Guard-4, OpenAI Moderation API (both Text-Moderation and Omni-Moderation), and find that the prompts can easily bypass these guardrails, posing a limited defense to diversity-driven TTS failure.\nL72: ## 2 Related Works\nL73: Test-Time Scaling. Recent work has demonstrated that strategic allocation of computational resources during inference can substantially improve LLM reasoning without modifying pre-trained parameters. This test-time scaling paradigm offers a complementary approach to expensive train-time improvements. Prompt-based methods enhance reasoning through strategic prompting.\nL74: Chain-of-Thought (CoT) (cite35†Wei et al., 2022 ) prompting generates intermediate reasoning steps, with Self-Consistency (cite40†Wang et al., 2022 ) extending this by sampling diverse reasoning paths and using majority voting. Tree-of-Thought (cite34†Yao et al., 2023 ) and Forest-of-Thought (cite41†Bi et al., 2024 ) further structure reasoning into trees with branch selection and self-correction. Search and verification methods explore multiple candidate solutions through sampling and ranking.\nL75: Best-of-N sampling (cite36†Cobbe et al., 2021 ; cite42†Lightman et al., 2023 ) and Monte Carlo Tree Search (cite37†Coulom, 2006 ; cite38†Gao et al., 2024 ) demonstrate particular success on mathematical reasoning (cite43†Xie et al., 2024a ). s1 (cite44†Muennighoff et al., 2025 ) acheived high performance using reasoning traces of only 1000 samples.\nL76: Ensembling strategies leverage complementary strengths: PackLLM (cite45†Mavromatis et al., 2024 ) uses perplexity-based weighting for test-time model fusion, and LE-MCTS (cite46†Park et al., 2024 ) enables process-level ensemble where models collaboratively build solutions step-by-step. Iterative refinement allows models to self-correct. Self-Refine (cite47†Madaan et al., 2023 ) achieves improvement through iterative critique and revision.\nL77: Retrieval-augmented approaches like IRCoT (cite48†Trivedi et al., 2022 ) interleave reasoning with dynamic information retrieval, improving multi-hop QA while reducing hallucination. Additionally, calibration methods like Adaptive Temperature Scaling (cite49†Xie et al., 2024b ) provide token-level temperature adjustment to maintain well-calibrated confidence estimates.\nL78: Robustness of LLMs. The robustness landscape of LLMs has evolved from simple prompt manipulation to sophisticated strategies targeting reasoning mechanisms that reveal critical failures. Early foundational work included Greedy Coordinate Gradient (GCG) (cite50†Zou et al., 2023a ) which introduced gradient-based optimization for adversarial suffixes. PAIR (cite51†Chao et al., 2024 ) pioneered the LLM-as-adversary paradigm, requiring only 20 queries versus hundreds for gradient methods.\nL79: The AutoDAN family of attacks (cite52†Liu et al., 2024a ; cite53†Liu et al., 2024b ) advanced automated adversarial string generation through genetic algorithms and lifelong learning. Other techniques expose architectural failure models in differing manners. FlipAttack (cite54†Liu et al., 2024c ) achieves success by manipulating the order of autoregressive processing, while ArtPrompt (cite55†Jiang et al., 2024 ) uses ASCII art to exploit visual-semantic processing gaps.\nL80: Systematic approaches include ReNeLLM (cite56†Ding et al., 2023 ) for generalized prompt rewriting and scenario nesting, DeepInception (cite57†Li et al., 2023 ) for manipulation by taking advantage of the personification capabilities of an LLM, and Tree of Attacks (cite58†Mehrotra et al., 2024 ) which achieves success using fewer queries through systematic exploration of the outputs of an Attacker-LLM.\nL81: Preemptive Answer attacks (cite59†Xu et al., 2024 ) inject fabricated answers before reasoning begins, assessing the robustness of the model’s reasoning capability across various CoT methods. OverThink (cite60†Kumar et al., 2025 ) introduces resource exhaustion attacks achieving slowdowns forcing excessive computation.\nL82: Recently robutness research has also pivoted to large reasoning models, demonstrating effectiveness: Mousetrap (cite61†Yao et al., 2025 ) achieves success through iterative prompt transformations, AutoRAN (cite62†Liang et al., 2025 ) uses smaller, less-aligned reasoning models as an adversary for the larger target reasoning models. Hijacking Chain-of-Thought (H-CoT) (cite63†Kuo et al., 2025 ) reduces refusal rates by hijacking visible reasoning processes across large open-source reasoning models.\nL83: ## 3 Problem Statement and Proposed Stress Test\nL84: ### 3.1 Preliminaries\nL85: \nL86: LLMs. Let $\\mathcal{V}$ denote a finite vocabulary of tokens, and let $\\mathcal{X}\\subseteq\\mathcal{V}^{*}$ denote the input space of natural language prompts. A large language model (LLM) $\\mathcal{M}$ defines an autoregressive probability distribution over output sequences $y=(y_{1},\\dots,y_{K})\\in\\mathcal{V}^{*}$ given an input $x\\in\\mathcal{X}$:\nL87: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Model’s Language Matters: A Comparative Privacy Analysis of LLMs (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.08813v1)\nciteturn2816view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.08813v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 301\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStronger Re-identification Attacks through Reasoning and Aggregation (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09184v1)\nciteturn2816view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09184v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 330\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDetecting Data Contamination from Reinforcement Learning Post-training for Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09259v1)\nciteturn2816view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09259v1\",